        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="16">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.16</English>
                <French>v1.16</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

With a measured 4.32 air changes per hour at 50Pa during the blower door test, your house is about average when it comes to air leakage. In addition, because of the warm weather on the day of the evaluation I was unable to get any good photos using the IR camera.



I did note some significant air leakage coming from below the frame on the leftmost window in your daughter's room. I did spot ckeck other windows, but did not find this air leakage anywhere else. 



Other upgrades recommended in this report may also affect your air leakage rate.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $270&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;9.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house are 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation, with 1.5 inches of rigid XPS insulation on the exterior (R7.5). I have modelled adding another1.5 inches (R7.5) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation to the outside of your walls.



Since your exterior siding is in good physical condition, and you mentioned that you do not have plans to replace it at this current time, I have given this upgrade a much lower priority. When you do plan on redoing the siding and upgrading your current exterior insulation, contact the Arctic Energy Alliance for up to date best practices and available rebates.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R7.5 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $195&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;6.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <windows>

As discussed, all of your windows are double pane windows that appear to have a Low E coating and fill gas. Since you are planning on upgrading your windows, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



The windows I modelled to get the savings estimate below have a double low E solar stop coating. As we discussed, you may want to replace your windows strategically instead of replacing them all at once with the same thing. Remember that since Yellowknife is a heating dominated climate, there's a trade off between preventing overheating in the summer and taking advantage of solar gain during the winter. 



Hinged windows provide a better air seal when closed and latched than other types of windows.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all your windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $310&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;10.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</windows>
                <doors>

All your upstairs exterior doorsr appear to be in good physical condition. I did notice some damage to the weather stripping around the front and back doors, so you may wish to consider replacing the weather stripping to eliminate drafts. 



Since you did mention possibly replacing your doors for other reasons, if you do decide to replace any of your doors choose ENERGY STAR® qualified patio door rated for climate zone 3. Also, pay close attention to the air sealing around the door itself while installing it - as with windows, a continuous seal around the frame will prevent air leakage, which can be a significant source of heat loss. 



The door to your basement is a solid wood door. I have modelled replacing it with an ENERGY STAR® qualified patio door rated for climate zone 3.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your basement door with ENERGY STAR® qualified door rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $25&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;1.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Currently, your hot water is heated from your boiler. Because your oil boiler already has an AFUE of 86.2%, I have modelled replacing the boiler with an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. The savings for space heating are listed elsewhere in this report, but because you have a combi system for heat and hot water, an upgrade to your boiler will also result in energy savings for hot water.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your oil fired boiler with an ENERGY STAR® qualified propane fired boiler with an AFUE of 95%, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $240&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage related to your hot water heating by &lt;b&gt;7.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. Rebates for the boiler are listed elsewhere. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;







an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil fired boiler was originally rated at 86.2% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



Upgrading the boiler will also result in energy savings for domestic hot water. These savings are listed in the hot water section of the report. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $1,130&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;31.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>



Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely. The short and dark days of winter provide little or no usable energy, depending on the latitude in the NWT. However, the long hours of sunlight in spring and summer can provide usable heat and electricity for up to eight months of the year.  As prices continue to drop, solar PV panels are becoming competitive with the cost of diesel generation. 



Since you have a south facing roof with minimal shading around your house, I have modelled the impact a solar photovoltaic system would have on your electricity use.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $1,190&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;14.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$2,500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program. 



&lt;b&gt; GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system. You would be eligible for 1/3 of the purchase cost, up to a maximum of $2,500 per year. Applicants are strongly encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to learn the systems usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house you can qualify for &lt;b&gt;$ 175 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.12 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $5.67 per bag



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Your Energy Advisor&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



&lt;b&gt;Alexandra Giroux

Energy Management Specialist&lt;/b&gt;

